<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karesi Bey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. late 13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karesi Bey was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> leader who around the end of the thirteenth century took the Byzantine region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikesir">Balıkesir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> of Karesi. After his death his sons Demirhan and Yakhshi divided the lands and quarrelled, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> annexed the beylik in the 1340s, gaining the soldiers and ships to cross into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Former Byzantine lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> had come under the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum and other Islamic states after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071. In 1243 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> defeated Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a> and pushed Turkoman tribes towards the edges of the peninsula, including the Byzantine frontier. The Karasids were among the Turkish groups that moved into western Anatolia from the east. Karasi's father was Kalam, called Calames by Nicephorus Gregoras, and Karasi himself appears in Gregoras as Carases.</p><p>Epitaphs of 1415 for a Karasi family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a> trace their ancestry to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a>. İsmail Hakkı Uzunçarşılı notes the connection, but Claude Cahen thinks the shared name may not prove it, and Clifford Edmund Bosworth calls the theory almost certainly a tale. Elizabeth Zachariadou accepts Malik Danishmend as a remote ancestor and reads Karasi's grandfather's name as Baghdi, matching a ruler Pagdinis in George Pachymeres. She also observes that Karasi is not a Turkish name and may come from the town he controlled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Later Ottoman sources called Karasi a nöker, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Rum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesud-ii">Mesud II</a>, and said that he and Kalam took over the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikesir">Balıkesir</a> and then claimed independence at an unknown date. Doukas wrote that they appeared during the rule of the Byzantine Emperor Andronikos II. The land corresponded to the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>, but not the towns of Artaki, Pegae, Adramytion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and Dardanellia.</p><p>Cahen proposes that the state appeared much later, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-muntaner">Ramon Muntaner</a> and Pachymeres do not mention the Karasids. Karasi expanded into Lesser Mysia and reached south to the border of the Sarukhanids. In 1311 he supported the Sari Saltuk tribe, led by Ece Halil, in its clash with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and let the survivors take refuge in his realm.</p><p>Karasi died before 1328, when his son and successor Demir Khan made an agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Some researchers placed his death in 1334 or 1336 because of a ruler called Ajlan in Ottoman sources, but primary sources call Ajlan the son of Karasi, and Zerrin Günal identifies him with Karasi's son Yakhshi. A tomb dedicated to Karasi stands in the Mustafa Fakih neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikesir">Balıkesir</a>, close to the Pasha Mosque. It holds five more graves, reputed to be those of his sons, and its sarcophagus bears inscriptions in Kufic script.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karasi Bey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around the end of the 13th century the Turkmen leader <b>Karesi Bey</b> took the former Byzantine region of Mysia, with Balikesir and Pergamon, as Byzantine defences in western Anatolia collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/karasids">Karasids</a></li></ul></section>
